":false,"description":"For issues and PRs. A bug that does not happen in an earlier version of Sequelize."}],"state":"closed","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":7,"created_at":"2017-06-10T07:08:57Z","updated_at":"2017-06-28T11:23:03Z","closed_at":"2017-06-28T11:23:03Z","assignee":null,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"## What you are doing?\r\n\r\n```js\r\nconst Sequelize = require('sequelize');\r\nconst sequelize = new Sequelize('db', 'user', 'pass', {\r\n  host: 'localhost',\r\n  port: 5432,\r\n  dialect: 'postgres',\r\n});\r\n\r\nconst Test = sequelize.define(\r\n  'test', {\r\n    unique1: Sequelize.INTEGER,\r\n    // even unique; false doesn't override the effect\r\n    unique2: {type: Sequelize.INTEGER, unique: false},\r\n  }, {\r\n    indexes: [{\r\n      unique: true,\r\n      fields: ['unique1', 'unique2']\r\n    }]\r\n  },\r\n);\r\n\r\nTest.sync({force: true})\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\n## What do you expect to happen?\r\nPreviously ( `4.0.0-2` ) used to define **only** composite unique index. Which is the expected behavior.\r\n\r\n## What is actually happening?\r\nBut now it defines 3 unique indices:\r\n\r\n1. `UNIQUE (unique1)`\r\n2. `UNIQUE (unique2)`\r\n3. `UNIQUE (unique1, unique2)`\r\n\r\nI think the culprit is #7196.\r\n\r\nThis is obviously a regression as my queries are now broken, because `unique1` and `unique2` should be unique in combination not in their own.\r\n\r\n_Output, either JSON or SQL_\r\n\r\nConstraint check errors... 😱 😱 \r\n\r\n__Dialect:__ postgres (possibly any)\r\n__Database version:__ 9.6\r\n__Sequelize version:__ 4.0.0","closed_by":{"login":"felixfbecker","id":10532611,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEwNTMyNjEx","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/10532611?v=4","gravatar_i